{
  "answer_text": "Treat any server discovered from a public or custom registry as untrusted until vetted: validate endpoints (TLS, hostnames, signed manifests), confirm advertised capabilities/schemas and versions, and enforce strong authentication/authorization (short‑lived tokens or mTLS and least‑privilege scopes) before enabling functionality. At runtime apply sandboxing, execution and rate limits, allowlist/denylist and operator-approval flows, comprehensive logging/auditing and the ability to revoke or quarantine servers, and require explicit user/operator consent before sending PII or other sensitive data. These controls are necessary because registry discovery provides availability and discovery but not trust—servers may misbehave, differ in capabilities, or expose sensitive data if not properly constrained.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If a client or registry UI assumes the server always returns the latest-compatible versions or uses stale cached metadata, runtime incompatibilities can occur (schema/method changes, auth/contract changes, deserialization or method-not-found errors), pinned reproducible workloads can break, and clients may silently receive unsupported capabilities. Clients should explicitly request and validate version identifiers (use semver or explicit tags), expose and allow pinning to specific versions instead of showing only “latest,” perform schema validation and feature-gating based on declared capabilities, use cache TTLs and revalidation tied to server-provided version IDs, and treat custom endpoints as independent authorities that require full capability negotiation and version checks. These measures preserve reproducibility and reduce unexpected breakages from silent upgrades or stale metadata.",
  "source_quote": "Show only latest versions"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If the MCP Registry or its UI shows “Loading servers...” or a “Retry” option, clients should expect transient registry unavailability (timeouts, networking/DNS/TLS hiccups, rate-limiting or temporary outages) and design discovery to tolerate failures. Implement resilient behaviors such as bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter, local caching of previously validated registry entries for degraded/read-only operation, configurable fallback/pinned endpoints or staging registries, and fail-fast/circuit-breaker logic plus diagnostics/metrics to surface problems and avoid overloading the registry. For critical operations, consider conservative fail-closed semantics or operator approval before proceeding when registry validation is unavailable.",
  "source_quote": "Loading servers...\n\nRetry"
}

{
  "answer_text": "The client must treat the upstream MCP Registry as the canonical authoritative starting point but assume downstream sub-registries may transform, augment, or lag behind that canonical data. To avoid connecting to stale, removed, or conflicting server entries the client must implement reconciliation and freshness checks: per-entry versioning (timestamps/ETags/sequence numbers) and provenance metadata, ordered application of updates, validation of live reachability and TLS/API compatibility before use, and treating upstream removals/denylist signals as high-priority authoritative actions. Together these measures ensure the client prefers the most recent upstream authority, detects and resolves conflicts, and avoids exposing or connecting to stale or retired servers.",
  "source_quote": "Our goal is to standardize how servers are distributed and discovered, providing a primary source of truth that sub-registries can build upon."
}

{
  "answer_text": "If the Registry denylists and retroactively removes a server, clients must treat that server as removed/unavailable and stop using it for discovery or connections. Clients should update their local registry/caches to reflect the removal and surface the change to operators so any necessary remediation (for example, credential revocation or user notification) can be carried out.",
  "source_quote": "Registry maintainers can then denylist these entries and retroactively remove them from public access."
}

{
  "answer_text": "When switching between local (in‑process/IPC) and remote (networked) transports you must explicitly manage the connection lifecycle (handshake/establishment, timeouts, graceful close, reconnection with backoff) rather than relying on in‑process persistence. Do not assume FIFO or duplicate‑free delivery on the remote path: implement protocol‑level sequencing/acknowledgements, unique request IDs, idempotent operations and duplicate detection so retries are safe. Keep session state and ordering at the protocol layer (session resume or explicit state reconciliation/replay) and add observability so transient failures can be detected and recovered without leaving inconsistent session state.",
  "source_quote": "Local and remote transport protocols"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Keeping server and client roles separate enforces clear trust and operational boundaries: servers mediate access control, auditing, resource normalization, and backend integration while clients handle orchestration, UI, and agent logic. This separation enables reuse (many clients per server), least-privilege security, independent scaling and upgrades, and simpler auditing and tenant isolation. Collapsing them into a monolith creates tight coupling, increases blast radius and deployment/upgrade complexity, makes auditing, access control and tenant isolation harder, and complicates scaling, latency and fault-domain management.",
  "source_quote": "Create MCP servers to expose your data and tools"
}
